# Coach speech: the rules lesson (level 0, src/coach/lesson.cpp).
# English is the reference; every language has exactly these keys (and the same variants).
#
# Placeholders: {your} / {my} = a piece of the player / of the coach ("your rook", "my knight");
# {sq}, {sq2}, {sq3}, {to}, {esc} = squares ({to} = the square the player just moved to, {esc} = where the
# coach's king escapes); {move} = a move. {@}, {@2}, {@3} are invisible marks: the coach points at
# something while saying the word right after the mark, so keep each mark in front of the word
# that means the same thing in your sentence. A ".spoken" key is what the voice says instead
# (here: file letters spelled for the voice).

# ---- Chapter titles (said inside "Let's continue with {chapter}.") ------------------------------------
lesson.title.welcome = the board
lesson.title.pieces_lines = how the rook, bishop and queen move
lesson.title.pieces_steps = how the king, knight and pawn move
lesson.title.values_setup = piece values and the first moves
lesson.title.safety = safe and unsafe captures
lesson.title.check = check
lesson.title.mate = checkmate
lesson.title.castling = castling
lesson.title.en_passant = en passant
lesson.title.promotion = promotion
lesson.title.draws = stalemate and draws
lesson.title.before_move = before you move
lesson.title.opening = the opening
lesson.title.review = a quick review
lesson.title.etiquette = how our games work

# ---- Lines every exercise uses ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# After the coach demonstrated the solution.
lesson.now_you = Now you try.
lesson.now_you.2 = Your turn now.
lesson.now_you.3 = Now you.
# Another wrong move after the demonstration.
lesson.almost = Almost! Play the move I showed you.
lesson.almost.2 = Not quite. Try the move from my demonstration.
lesson.almost.3 = Close. Remember what I just showed you.
# A wrong move without a line of its own.
lesson.try_again = That's not it. Try again.
lesson.try_again.2 = Not this one. Have another go.
lesson.try_again.3 = Let's try once more.
lesson.use_your = Use {your}.
lesson.use_your.2 = Try it with {your}.
lesson.watch = Watch.
lesson.like_this = Like this.
lesson.and_back = And back.
# A check that is not checkmate (the coach then shows the escape).
lesson.check_escape = Check, but my king escapes to {esc}.
lesson.check_takes = Check, but my king simply takes {your}.
lesson.check_block = Check, but I can put a piece in the way.
lesson.stalemate = Stalemate! My king can't move, and it's not in check. A draw, though you were winning!

# ---- Why a move is not allowed (the coach points at the piece or square in question) ----------------
# {sq} = the square in question, {my} = the coach's piece that attacks, pins, blocks or gives
# check, {your} = the piece the player tried to move.
why.KingIntoCheck = Not {sq}: {my} attacks it. A king never steps into danger.
why.PathBlocked = Something is in the {@}way: pieces can't jump, except the knight.
why.WrongGeometry = {your} doesn't move like that.
why.MustAnswerCheck = You're in {@}check: you must deal with it first.
why.CastlingThroughCheck = Your king would cross {sq}, and {my} attacks it.
why.CastlingNoRights = That king or rook has already moved: no castling.
why.CastlingBlocked = Castling needs empty squares between the king and the rook.
why.CastlingOutOfCheck = You can't castle while in check.
why.PawnForwardBlocked = Pawns can't capture straight ahead: that square is {@}blocked.
why.LeavesKingInCheck = {your} is pinned: moving it would expose your king to {my}.
why.OwnPieceOnTarget = You can't land on your own piece.
why.PawnCaptureNeedsVictim = A pawn moves diagonally only to capture.
why.EnPassantExpired = En passant is only allowed right after the pawn's double step.
why.NotYourTurn = Wait: it's my turn.

# ---- Chapter 0: welcome and the board -------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.welcome.hello = Hello! I'm your coach. Let's learn the rules of chess together, one small step at a time.
lesson.welcome.board = This is the chessboard: sixty-four squares, light and dark, eight rows of eight.
lesson.welcome.light = The corner square on your {@}right is always a light square. Remember: light on the right.
lesson.welcome.files = Columns are called files. They have letters, from {@}a to {@2}h.
lesson.welcome.files.spoken = Columns are called files. They have letters, from {@}ay to {@2}aitch.
lesson.welcome.ranks = Rows are called ranks. They have numbers, from {@}one to {@2}eight, starting on your side.
lesson.welcome.square = Each square is named by its letter, then its number. This square is {sq}.
lesson.welcome.square2 = And this one is {sq}.
lesson.welcome.kings = These are the two kings. Yours is on {sq}, mine is on {sq2}.
lesson.welcome.ask = Your turn: move your king one square forward, to {sq}.
lesson.welcome.wrong = That's {to}. We want {sq}: letter e, number two.
lesson.welcome.wrong.spoken = That's {to}. We want {sq}: letter ee, number two.
lesson.welcome.ok = Perfect. Your king is on {sq}.

# ---- Chapters 1 and 2: how the pieces move and capture -----------------------------------------------------
# Rook
lesson.rook.intro = This is {your}. It moves in straight lines, along files and ranks, as far as it likes.
lesson.rook.lines = Up the file, {@}or along the rank.
lesson.rook.capture = To capture, a piece lands on an {@}enemy piece, which then leaves the board.
lesson.rook.ask = Capture {my} with your rook!
lesson.rook.short = Right direction! Keep going up the file, all the way to {my}.
lesson.rook.rank = That's along the rank. {my} is up the file.
lesson.rook.king = That's your king. Use {your}, in the corner.
lesson.rook.ok = Captured! {my} leaves the board.
# Pieces don't jump
lesson.jump.blocked = Pieces can't jump over others. Your pawn on {sq} blocks the rook's path.
lesson.jump.all = It's the same for the bishop and the queen: they can't jump over pieces either.
# Bishop
lesson.bishop.intro = This is {your}. It moves diagonally, as far as it likes.
lesson.bishop.colour = A bishop always stays on squares of the same colour.
lesson.bishop.ask = {my} stands on its diagonal. Capture it!
lesson.bishop.short = Right diagonal! Go further, until you reach {my}.
lesson.bishop.other = That's the other diagonal. Look toward {my}.
lesson.bishop.ok = Excellent. Bishops love long diagonals.
# Queen
lesson.queen.intro = This is {your}, the strongest piece. She moves like a rook and a bishop together.
lesson.queen.ask = Find {my} and capture it with your queen.
lesson.queen.wrong = Your queen can reach {my} in one move, along a diagonal.
lesson.queen.ok = Well done! Keep your queen safe: she's worth a lot.
# King
lesson.king.intro = The king moves just one square, in any direction.
lesson.king.important = The king is the most important piece. If it's trapped, the game is lost.
lesson.king.ask = The king can capture too. Take {my}!
lesson.king.wrong = Your king is right next to {my}. Step onto its square to capture it.
lesson.king.ok = Good! The king is slow, but strong up close.
# Knight
lesson.knight.intro = This is {your}. It moves in an L: two squares straight, then one to the side.
lesson.knight.l = Like {@}this. It always lands on a square of the other colour.
lesson.knight.jump = The knight is the only piece that can jump over others.
lesson.knight.ask = Capture {my} with your knight!
lesson.knight.wrong = That's a real knight move, but it doesn't reach {my}. Try another L.
lesson.knight.ok = Great jump!
# Pawn: moving
lesson.pawn.intro = Now {your}. It moves straight forward, one square at a time, and never backward.
lesson.pawn.first = On its first move, a pawn may go one or two squares.
lesson.pawn.ask = Move your pawn two squares forward.
lesson.pawn.one = That's one square: legal, but this time go two.
lesson.pawn.use = Use the pawn.
lesson.pawn.ok = Good.
# Pawn: capturing
lesson.pawncap.blocked = My pawn blocks yours. Pawns can't capture straight ahead.
lesson.pawncap.diagonal = But a pawn captures diagonally, one square forward.
lesson.pawncap.ask = Capture {my} with your pawn.
lesson.pawncap.use = Use {your}: it captures diagonally forward.
lesson.pawncap.ok = Yes! Straight to move, diagonal to capture.

# ---- Chapter 3: piece values, set-up and the first moves ----------------------------------------------------
lesson.values.pawn = Each piece has a value in points. A pawn is worth one.
lesson.values.pieces = A knight or a {@}bishop, three. A {@2}rook, five. The {@3}queen, nine.
lesson.values.king = The king is priceless: you can never give it away.
lesson.values.trade = Don't swap a bigger piece for a smaller one, unless you win more than you lose.
# Practice: two pieces nothing defends ({sq} = the queen's square, {sq2} = the pawn's, {my} = the queen)
lesson.values.two = Your knight attacks two of my pieces: {sq} and {sq2}. Nothing defends either of them.
lesson.values.ask = Take the one that is worth more!
lesson.values.small = That's a pawn, worth one. {my} was free too, and she's worth nine.
lesson.values.look = Look at what your knight attacks, and take the piece worth more.
lesson.values.ok = Yes! A queen is worth nine. When two pieces are free, take the bigger one.
lesson.setup.corners = Every game starts like this: rooks in the {@}corners, then {@2}knights, then {@3}bishops.
lesson.setup.queen = The queen stands on her own colour: your queen is on a light square.
lesson.setup.king = The king stands next to her. White always moves first.
lesson.first.ask = Start the game: move the pawn in front of your king two squares, to {sq}.
lesson.first.wrong = That's a legal move too! For now, please play your king's pawn to {sq}.
lesson.first.ok = A great first move: it opens lines for your queen and bishop.
lesson.first.reply = I answer the same way. Now bring out a knight: jump from {sq} to {sq2}.
lesson.first.knight_wrong = Knights jump over the pawns. Try from {sq} to {sq2}.
lesson.first.knight_ok = Perfect. Knights and bishops come out early.

# ---- Chapter 4: safe and unsafe captures ---------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.safety.defended = A piece is defended when a friend can capture back.
lesson.safety.pawn = My {@}pawn defends {my}.
lesson.safety.free = But {my} has no defender. Your queen attacks both pieces.
lesson.safety.ask = Capture the free piece!
lesson.safety.watch = Watch what happens.
lesson.safety.gave = You gave your queen, worth nine, for my knight, worth three.
lesson.safety.other = Try the other piece.
lesson.safety.look = Look for the piece that nothing defends.
lesson.safety.ok = Excellent! Always ask: is that piece defended?
# A defended pawn: the queen must not take it ({sq}, {sq2} = the two pawns)
lesson.safety.pawns = Your queen attacks two of my pawns: {sq} and {sq2}.
lesson.safety.pawns_ask = Capture the pawn that is safe to take.
lesson.safety.gave_pawn = You gave your queen, worth nine, for a pawn, worth one.
lesson.safety.pawns_other = Even a pawn can be defended. Try the other pawn.
lesson.safety.pawns_ok = Right! Never risk your queen for a defended pawn.
# The other way round: a small piece may take a big defended one ({my} = the queen)
lesson.safety.small = Now the other way round. {my} is defended, but your pawn attacks her.
lesson.safety.small_count = If you take her, my {@}pawn takes yours back. You give one point and win nine.
lesson.safety.small_ask = Capture my queen with the right piece!
lesson.safety.small_other = Take her with your pawn instead: the cheapest piece that can.
lesson.safety.small_look = Your pawn attacks my queen. Capture her with it.
lesson.safety.small_ok = Yes! A small piece may take a big one, even a defended one: you lose one point and win nine.
lesson.safety.count = Before every capture, count what you win and what you could lose back.

# ---- Chapter 5: check and getting out of check -----------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.danger.rank = My rook controls the whole second rank.
lesson.danger.rule = A king may never move to a square where it could be captured.
lesson.danger.ask = Move your king to a safe square.
lesson.danger.ok = Right. Your king is safe there.
lesson.check.what = My rook attacks your king. That's called check.
lesson.check.must = When you're in check, you must get out of it right away.
lesson.check.ways = There are three ways: {@}move the king, {@2}block the attack, or {@3}capture the attacker.
lesson.check.ask = Here, move your king out of check.
lesson.check.file = {to} is still on {@}my rook's file. Step off the line.
lesson.check.ok = Good: you're out of check.
lesson.block.again = Check again! This time your king has no free square.
lesson.block.ask = So block the attack: put a piece in the way.
lesson.block.queen = That blocks! But my rook could take your queen. Block with a cheaper piece.
lesson.block.queen_ok = It works. Remember: block with your cheapest piece.
lesson.block.ok = Perfect. Block with your least valuable piece.
lesson.capture.check = My queen gives check, right next to your king.
lesson.capture.ask = Capture the attacker!
lesson.capture.king_ok = Yes! My queen had no defender, so your king could take it.
lesson.capture.knight_ok = Yes! Your knight removed the attacker.
lesson.check.open.ask = Check again! This time get out of it any way you like.
lesson.check.open.king = You moved the king away. A piece could also have blocked, or your knight could have taken my rook.
lesson.check.open.block = You blocked the attack. Your king could also have stepped aside, or your knight could have taken my rook.
lesson.check.open.take = You took my rook, the piece giving check. Moving the king or blocking would have worked too.
lesson.check.open.hint = Three ways: {@}move the king, {@2}block on the file, or {@3}capture my rook with your knight.

# ---- Chapter 6: checkmate ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.mate1.what = Checkmate: the king is in check, and it has no way out. That wins the game.
lesson.mate1.stuck = My king is stuck behind its {@}own pawns.
lesson.mate1.ask = Checkmate me in one move!
lesson.mate1.wrong = Close! Give check on my back rank, where my king can't escape.
lesson.mate1.ok = Checkmate! That's the back-rank mate. Remember it!
lesson.mate1.king = Your king is fine where it is. Use the rook to give check.
lesson.mate1.why = See? The rook covers my {@}whole back rank, and my {@2}own pawns fill the squares in front of my king.
lesson.mate2.ask = Your king and queen work as a team. Checkmate me in one move.
lesson.mate2.wrong = Find a check that leaves my king no square at all.
lesson.mate2.ok = Checkmate! Your king protects the queen, so mine can't take her.
lesson.mate2.ok_rank = Checkmate on the back rank! Your king guards every square in front of mine.
lesson.mate2.king = Your king guards the squares next to mine. Leave it there and use the queen.
lesson.mate2.half = That watches {sq}, but my king can still go to {sq2}.
lesson.mate2.why = See? Your queen covers {sq} and {sq2}, and your king covers the squares in front of mine. My king has no way out.
lesson.mate3.edge = Your rook on the seventh rank keeps my king on the edge.
lesson.mate3.ask = Now use {your} to checkmate.
lesson.mate3.free = Check, but the seventh rank is free now, and my king escapes.
lesson.mate3.takes = My king simply takes your rook.
lesson.mate3.hint = Give check on the back rank with the rook that isn't guarding the seventh.
lesson.mate3.ok = Checkmate! The two rooks worked like a ladder.
lesson.mate3.why = See? One rook cuts off the {@}seventh rank, and the other gives check on the {@2}back rank. My king has no way out.
lesson.mate4.look = Check is not always checkmate. Let's see what happens.
lesson.mate4.ask = Give my king check with your rook.
lesson.mate4.king = Your king stays where it is. Use the rook to give check.
lesson.mate4.wrong = That's not check. Your rook can attack my king from the back rank.
lesson.mate4.ok = Yes, that's check! But is it checkmate? Watch.
lesson.mate4.after = My pawn on {sq} gave my king room. Before you celebrate a check, count the squares my king can still use.
lesson.mate5.ask = Two of your rook moves give check, and only one is checkmate. Count my king's squares, then choose.
lesson.mate5.king = Your king already covers {sq} and {sq2}. Leave it there and move the rook.
lesson.mate5.wrong = That's not check. Look for a rook move that attacks my king.
lesson.mate5.hint = Your king covers {sq} and {sq2}. Which rook check also takes away {sq3}?
lesson.mate5.why = See? The rook covers {sq}, your king covers the other two squares, and my king is stuck.
lesson.mate5.ok = Checkmate! You counted my king's squares: none were left.

# ---- Chapter 7: castling ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.castle.what = Castling is a special move that keeps your king safe. King and rook move together.
lesson.castle.how = The {@}king moves two squares toward a rook, and the {@2}rook jumps to its other side.
lesson.castle.ask = Always touch the king first. Now you: castle on your king's side.
lesson.castle.rook = That only moves the rook. To castle, move your king two squares toward it.
lesson.castle.long = That's queen-side castling, also legal! This time, castle toward the nearer rook.
lesson.castle.one = One square is a normal king move. For castling, the king moves two.
lesson.castle.other = To castle, move your king two squares toward the rook on {sq}.
lesson.castle.ok = Castled! Your king is tucked away, and your rook is ready to play.
lesson.castle2.moved = You can't castle if your king or that rook has already moved.
lesson.castle2.attacked = Nor while in check, or if your king would cross or land on an attacked square.
lesson.castle2.bishop = My bishop attacks {sq}. So castling on this side is forbidden now.
lesson.castle2.ask = Castle on the other side.
lesson.castle2.other = Castle toward your other rook, on the queen's side.
lesson.castle2.ok = Correct! Queen-side castling puts your king on {sq}.

# ---- Chapter 8: en passant ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.ep0.rule = En passant starts with the double step. Only a pawn {@}still at home may go two squares. A pawn that {@2}has moved goes one at a time.
lesson.ep0.ask = Move one of your pawns two squares.
lesson.ep0.moved = That pawn has already moved, so it can only go one square. Use the pawn that's still at home.
lesson.ep0.ok = Right: only a pawn on its starting square may go two.
lesson.ep.what = One special pawn capture is called en passant. It means "in passing".
lesson.ep.watch = Watch my pawn: it moves two squares and lands right beside yours.
lesson.ep.how = Your pawn may capture it as if it had moved only one square: to {sq}.
lesson.ep.now = But only right now, on this move. Capture en passant!
lesson.ep.gone = The chance is gone: en passant only works right away. Let's try again.
lesson.ep.ok = En passant! My pawn on {sq} is removed.
lesson.ep2.old = My pawn on {sq} has stood beside yours for a while. It's too late to capture it en passant.
lesson.ep2.watch = Now watch my other pawn.
lesson.ep2.ask = Two of my pawns stand beside yours, but only one just moved two squares. Capture that one en passant!
lesson.ep2.push = That only pushes your pawn. Capture the pawn that just moved two squares.
lesson.ep2.wrong = Look for the pawn that just made its double step, and capture it en passant.
lesson.ep2.hint = My pawn on {sq} just moved two squares. Capture it en passant, to {sq2}.
lesson.ep2.ok = En passant! My pawn on {sq} could not be taken: its chance was over.

# ---- Chapter 9: promotion -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.promo.what = When a pawn reaches the last rank, it's promoted: it becomes a queen, rook, bishop or knight.
lesson.promo.ask = Almost always, choose a queen. Promote your pawn now!
lesson.promo.under = Allowed! But a queen is stronger. Let's pick the queen.
lesson.promo.under_ok = Fine. Most players choose the queen, though.
lesson.promo.push = Push your pawn to the last rank, to {sq}.
lesson.promo.ok = A new queen! You can even have two queens at once.
lesson.promo2.ask = A pawn can also promote by capturing, diagonally. Take my rook and make a queen!
lesson.promo2.push = That promotes too, but my rook guards {sq}. Watch.
lesson.promo2.lost = My rook took your new piece. Capture my rook first.
lesson.promo2.wrong = That doesn't capture my rook. Your pawn takes diagonally, on {sq}.
lesson.promo2.hint = Take my rook with your pawn, onto {sq}: it promotes as it captures.
lesson.promo2.ok = Captured and promoted! A pawn can do both in one move.

# ---- Chapter 10: stalemate and draws -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.stale.what = It's my turn. My king is not in check, but {@}every square around it is attacked.
lesson.stale.draw = I have no legal move. That's stalemate, and the game is a draw.
lesson.stale2.ask = When you're winning, beware of stalemate. Checkmate me in one move, without stalemate!
lesson.stale2.wrong = Find a check that leaves my king no square. Careful: a quiet move can stalemate me.
lesson.stale2.king = Your king already covers {sq} and {sq2}. Leave it there and use the queen.
lesson.stale2.hint = Your king covers {sq} and {sq2}. Check me along the back rank, and my king has nowhere to go.
lesson.stale2.why = See? My king is attacked {@}and has no free square: that's checkmate. Without the check, it would be stalemate.
lesson.stale2.ok = Checkmate! Always leave the enemy king a move, unless you're giving mate.
lesson.draws.kings = Other draws: when nobody can checkmate, like two kings alone, the game is a draw.
lesson.draws.agree = Players can also agree to a draw.
lesson.draws.repeat = Repeating a position three times, or fifty moves without a capture or pawn move, allows a draw too.

# ---- Chapter 11: before you move (the safety habit) ---------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.before.habit = Before every move, stop and look. Strong players do it every time.
lesson.before.last = First question: what did my last move do? Watch.
lesson.before.threat = {my} attacks {your}. That's a threat.
lesson.before.safe = Moving away is not enough. The new square must be safe too.
lesson.before.dodge.ask = Move your knight to a safe square.
lesson.before.dodge.still = Your knight is still attacked. Watch.
lesson.before.dodge.hint = My pawns guard {sq} and {sq2}. Choose another square.
lesson.before.dodge.ok = Safe! You saw the threat, and you checked the square.
# A wrong move onto a guarded square ({my} guards {to}); the coach then takes the piece.
lesson.before.unsafe = Not {to}: {my} guards that square. Watch.
# Said after the coach took the piece, before the move is taken back.
lesson.before.lost = {your} is lost. Look first, then move.
lesson.before.again = Again, my move first. Watch.
lesson.before.free.ask = Look at my last move. Can you capture something for free?
lesson.before.free.missed = Look at {my} again. What can capture it?
lesson.before.free.hint = {my} has no defender. Your {@}pawn can take it.
lesson.before.free.ok = Yes! My bishop was free. Always look at my last move.
lesson.before.check.ask = Give me check, on a square I can't capture.
lesson.before.check.wrong = That's not check. Find a check on a safe square.
lesson.before.check.hint = {my} guards {sq}. Give check from a safe square.
lesson.before.check.ok = Good check! Your queen is safe there.
lesson.before.done = Make it a habit: look first, then move.

# ---- Chapter 12: the opening ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
lesson.opening.what = The opening is the start of the game. A few simple rules help.
lesson.opening.goals = {@}Take the centre, {@2}bring out knights and bishops, and {@3}castle early.
lesson.opening.centre = The four squares in the {@}middle are the centre.
lesson.opening.pawn.ask = Put a pawn in the centre: two squares forward.
lesson.opening.pawn.one = Not bad, but two squares take more of the centre.
lesson.opening.pawn.knight = Knights come soon. First, a pawn in the centre.
lesson.opening.pawn.wrong = Use the pawn in front of your king or your queen.
lesson.opening.pawn.ok = Good! Your pawn takes space in the centre.
lesson.opening.queen = Don't bring your queen out early: my pieces would chase her.
lesson.opening.knight.ask = Bring out a knight, toward the centre.
lesson.opening.rim = A knight on the edge reaches few squares. Jump toward the centre.
lesson.opening.bishop = Developing, good! But this time, a knight.
lesson.opening.knight.ok = Good! From there your knight watches the centre.
lesson.opening.later = A few moves later: your knight and {@}bishop are out.
lesson.opening.twice = Don't move the same piece twice without a good reason.
lesson.opening.castle.ask = The way is clear. Castle your king to safety.
lesson.opening.castle.wrong = Your king is ready. Castle now, while the way is clear.
lesson.opening.castle.ok = Castled! Your king is safe, and your rook joins the game.
lesson.opening.done = Centre, pieces, castle: that's a good start to every game.

# ---- Chapter 13: review (no teaching: the coach only asks; the positions come in a different order than the chapters) ----
lesson.review.start = Quick review, in a different order this time. I won't explain: you show me.
lesson.review.promo.ask = Promote this pawn.
lesson.review.promo.ok = Good: a new queen, and a check!
lesson.review.check.ask = Your king is in check. Get out of it.
lesson.review.check.ok = Out of check. Good.
lesson.review.castle.ask = Castle your king, on the side where it's allowed.
lesson.review.castle.other = That's not castling. Castle on the side where it's allowed.
lesson.review.castle.ok = Castled, on the safe side!
lesson.review.ep.ask = Capture my pawn en passant.
lesson.review.ep.ok = En passant! You remembered.
lesson.review.mate.ask = Checkmate me in one move.
lesson.review.mate.ok = Checkmate! Review done.

# ---- Chapter 14: how our games work (no clock, touch-move, handshake, and how the coach helps) -------------------
lesson.talk.rules = You now know every rule of chess. Well done!
lesson.talk.real = Let me tell you how our games will work.
lesson.talk.no_clock = With me, there's no clock and no rating. Take all the time you need to think.
lesson.talk.touch = A piece you touch must be moved, so look first, then touch.
lesson.talk.handshake = Games start and end with a handshake. It means: good game, and thank you.
lesson.talk.polite = Win or lose, stay polite, and never disturb your opponent while they think.
lesson.talk.help = In our games I'll help you. If one of your pieces is attacked or left unprotected, I'll tell you.
lesson.talk.help_back = After a big mistake, I'll show you why and offer to take your move back.
lesson.talk.help_skip = And if I talk too much, press Space: I'll stop.
lesson.talk.next = When you're ready, play First steps. I'll play gently.
lesson.talk.welcome = Welcome to chess! Let's shake hands.
